    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Many women notice their belly button flattening or popping into an "outie" as pregnancy progresses, most commonly around week 26, though the timing varies widely. It\'s completely harmless, doesn\'t affect your baby, and usually returns to its original shape within a few months after delivery.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Your belly button sits over a naturally thinner part of your abdominal wall, and as your womb expands and pushes outward, there\'s little muscle there to resist the pressure, so the navel gets pushed out. Skin elasticity, your body shape, and whether this is your first pregnancy all influence whether and when this happens.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There\'s nothing to do about it, and not every woman experiences a full "pop," some simply flatten out, which is equally normal. If it rubs uncomfortably against clothing, a soft bandage or looser clothing over the area can help.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention any pain, swelling, redness, or a bulge that doesn\'t resolve after delivery, since these occasionally point to an umbilical hernia rather than ordinary stretching.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'It\'s a small, almost funny detail in the grand scheme of pregnancy, but a good reminder of just how much your body reorganises itself to make room.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Belly buttons often flatten or pop out, commonly around week 26.',
        'Caused by the womb pushing against a naturally thin part of the abdomen.',
