    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Pelvic girdle pain is an ongoing ache around your pelvic joints, at the front, back, or both, caused by these joints loosening more than usual during pregnancy. It\'s different from round ligament pain, which is a brief, sharp catch from a stretching ligament; pelvic girdle pain tends to be more constant and affects movement like walking or turning in bed.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'A hormone called relaxin loosens your pelvic ligaments to help your body prepare for birth, but in some women this causes the joints to move a little too much, leading to instability and pain. This can affect the joint at the front of your pelvis, the back, or both, and is thought to affect around 1 in 5 pregnant women to some degree.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'A physiotherapist experienced in pregnancy-related pelvic pain can make a real difference; ask your doctor for a referral rather than assuming you must simply cope with it. In the meantime, keeping movements symmetrical, avoiding standing on one leg, using a pillow between your knees at night, and a support belt can all help.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Tell your doctor if pelvic pain is affecting your ability to walk, climb stairs, or move comfortably; a physiotherapy referral is a standard, effective next step.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This pain doesn\'t harm your baby, but it\'s genuinely worth taking seriously for your own comfort; you don\'t need to simply endure it until delivery.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Pelvic girdle pain is ongoing ache from loosened pelvic joints.',
        'Different from round ligament pain, which is brief and sharp.',
