  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BABY-017',
    number: 187,
    month: 4,
    question: 'How does my baby "borrow" my immunity before birth?',
    category: 'Baby Development',
    applicability: 'Month 4 onward, increasing through third trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'From around this point in pregnancy, and increasingly through the third trimester, your placenta actively passes a specific type of antibody, called IgG, from your blood into your baby\'s. This gives your newborn temporary, borrowed protection against infections you\'ve already fought off or been vaccinated against.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'IgG is the only antibody type able to cross the placenta this way, and your body sends it across deliberately, not by accident. This is one reason vaccines you receive during pregnancy, like the flu vaccine, can also help protect your baby directly in their first months, since your antibodies pass along too.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'This natural process is part of why staying up to date with any vaccines your doctor recommends during pregnancy matters, not just for you but for your baby\'s earliest protection. This borrowed immunity is temporary and fades over the first few months after birth, which is also why your baby\'s own vaccination schedule begins early in infancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor which vaccines, if any, are recommended for you during this pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Long before your baby meets a single germ, you\'re already quietly handing them a starter kit of protection, built from everything your own body has learned to fight.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The placenta actively transfers IgG antibodies from you to your baby.',
        'This gives temporary, borrowed protection after birth.',
        'Vaccines you receive in pregnancy can protect your baby directly too.',
        'Transfer increases through the third trimester.',
        'This protection fades over the first few months after birth.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BABY-015',
      'PREG-HEALTH-038',
      'PREG-HEALTH-026',
