    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Placental abruption is when the placenta separates from the womb wall before delivery, a different and separate condition from placenta previa, where the placenta sits low but stays attached. The key difference in how they feel: placenta previa typically causes painless bleeding, while abruption usually causes sudden, often severe pain along with a firm, tender belly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'In abruption, the placenta\'s attachment to the womb wall tears away, which can happen due to high blood pressure, trauma to the belly, smoking, or carrying twins, though the exact trigger isn\'t always clear. Because the placenta is your baby\'s only source of oxygen, this separation is a genuine emergency, needing prompt hospital assessment rather than a wait-and-see approach.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Any sudden, severe abdominal pain in the second half of pregnancy, with or without visible bleeding, needs urgent medical attention; some bleeding in abruption can be hidden internally rather than visible. This is different from the painless bleeding pattern of placenta previa, though your doctor will confirm which is happening through examination and scan.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Seek immediate medical care for sudden, severe abdominal pain, a hard or tender belly, or any bleeding in the second half of pregnancy; this isn\'t something to wait out at home.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Knowing the difference between these two isn\'t about worry, it\'s about knowing exactly what to do if something feels seriously wrong, which is itself a form of preparedness.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Abruption is placental separation; previa is a low placental position.',
        'Abruption usually causes pain and a firm belly; previa usually doesn\'t.',
        'Risk factors include high blood pressure, trauma, smoking, and twins.',
        'Abruption is a genuine emergency needing prompt hospital care.',
        'Bleeding in abruption can sometimes be hidden internally.',
      ]),
