        'Normally the cervix stays firm and closed until labour begins; in cervical insufficiency, the tissue is weaker than usual and can open under the ordinary weight of a growing pregnancy, without any labour signs like contractions. It\'s often only suspected after a previous unexplained second-trimester loss, or found incidentally during a routine cervical length scan.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you have risk factors, like a previous second-trimester loss or prior cervical surgery, your doctor may monitor your cervix more closely with scans through this trimester. If insufficiency is diagnosed, a cerclage is a well-established, effective treatment, often done as a same-day procedure, alongside possible hormone support your doctor may discuss.',
