    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Your blood pressure is checked at every visit to catch preeclampsia early, a pregnancy condition involving new high blood pressure, usually after 20 weeks, sometimes alongside protein in your urine or other organ effects. It\'s checked routinely precisely because many women have no symptoms at all until it\'s found this way.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Preeclampsia affects roughly 2 to 7 percent of pregnancies and involves changes to blood vessels that can affect your kidneys, liver, and the blood supply to your placenta. Catching it early through routine checks allows your doctor to monitor and manage it well before it becomes more serious, which is exactly why this simple check happens every single visit rather than only if you feel unwell.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'When symptoms do occur, they can include a persistent headache, vision changes like blurriness or flashing lights, sudden swelling in your hands or face, or pain under your ribs on the right side. None of these alone confirms preeclampsia, but any of them are worth reporting rather than waiting for your next scheduled visit.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Contact your doctor promptly for a severe headache, vision changes, sudden swelling, or upper abdominal pain, rather than waiting until your next appointment.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This is one of pregnancy\'s best examples of prevention through simple routine, a blood pressure cuff catching something that might otherwise go unnoticed until it mattered more.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Preeclampsia means new high blood pressure, usually after 20 weeks.',
        'Affects roughly 2 to 7 percent of pregnancies.',
        'Many women have no symptoms, which is why routine checks matter.',
        'Watch for headache, vision changes, swelling, or upper abdominal pain.',
        'Report these promptly rather than waiting for your next visit.',
      ]),
    ],
